BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (The Berkeley Fast File System was designed by Marshall Kirk McKusick and colleagues in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (early 1980s to overcome severe fragmentation and performance limits of earlier Unix) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (filesystems on magnetic disks. Their 1984 paper, A Fast File System for UNIX, documents the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (engineering rationale. UFS1 represents the classic on-disk inode and cylinder-group family) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (used by BSD-derived systems.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 582.0 Tm (UFS1 grew through BSD and FreeBSD evolution, with soft updates later providing) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (dependency-ordered metadata changes and optional journalling mechanisms appearing in some) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (operating systems. UFS2 introduced a new format for larger volumes and more metadata) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (capacity. UFS1 and UFS2 share conceptual architecture but not interchangeable on-disk inode) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 514.0 Tm (structures.) Tj ET
